WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.585
Modifiers

00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:05.450
An intro phrase describes the first noun after the comma.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:07.740
How to spot it

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.308
A describing phrase, then a comma

00:00:10.858 --> 00:00:13.086
Choices starting with different nouns

00:00:13.636 --> 00:00:18.155
It’s testing: whether a description sits next to what it describes

00:00:19.355 --> 00:00:20.121
The method

00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:23.063
Step 1: Read the intro phrase

00:00:23.343 --> 00:00:25.125
Who or what is it describing?

00:00:25.675 --> 00:00:28.358
Step 2: Put that noun first

00:00:28.638 --> 00:00:30.108
Right after the comma.

00:00:30.658 --> 00:00:32.627
Step 3: Watch possessives

00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:36.022
“Morrison’s novels” makes “novels” the subject.

00:00:36.572 --> 00:00:37.666
In a few words

00:00:37.816 --> 00:00:40.363
Who’s described? Put them first.

00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:42.344
The rules

00:00:42.744 --> 00:00:44.865
Intro phrase → first noun

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:47.527
Wrong: Walking to school, the rain started.

00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:50.763
Right: Walking to school, I got caught in the rain.

00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:53.118
Whoever did it comes first

00:00:53.398 --> 00:00:57.656
Wrong: After testing the samples, a conclusion was reached.

00:00:57.936 --> 00:01:02.168
Right: After testing the samples, the researchers concluded…

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:04.610
Possessives shift the subject

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.156
Wrong: Born in 1939, Carrera’s novels…

00:01:08.436 --> 00:01:12.313
Right: Born in 1939, Carrera wrote novels.

00:01:13.513 --> 00:01:14.574
Let’s try one

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:22.112
Born in Guatemala in 1939, ______ wrote novels and essays that explored the experiences of Indigenous

00:01:22.112 --> 00:01:23.069
communities.

00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:28.306
Born in Guatemala in 1939 describes a person.

00:01:28.856 --> 00:01:33.840
The work wasn’t born. Her novels weren’t born. It wasn’t born.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.513
Margarita Carrera was born, so her name goes right after the comma. Choice C.

00:01:40.713 --> 00:01:41.782
Common traps

00:01:42.182 --> 00:01:43.210
The possessive

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.006
The name is there, but it isn’t the subject.

00:01:46.556 --> 00:01:47.613
Passive voice

00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:49.933
“…was made by” hides who did it.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.118
Modifiers

00:01:52.398 --> 00:01:54.945
Who’s described? Put them first.

00:01:55.495 --> 00:01:56.075
Spot it

00:01:56.275 --> 00:02:00.750
Describing phrase + comma; choices start with different nouns

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.007
Steps

00:02:02.207 --> 00:02:04.597
1. Who or what does the intro describe?

00:02:04.877 --> 00:02:07.323
2. Put that noun right after the comma

00:02:07.603 --> 00:02:09.571
3. Watch out for possessives

00:02:10.121 --> 00:02:10.846
Remember

00:02:11.046 --> 00:02:13.965
Intro phrase → first noun after the comma

00:02:14.245 --> 00:02:16.051
Whoever did it comes first

00:02:16.331 --> 00:02:20.138
Morrison’s novels → the subject is “novels”

00:02:20.688 --> 00:02:21.129
Trap

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:23.524
The right name, but as a possessive

